**Corollary 7.4 (justified asymptotic direction).** For $n=4s$, $H=2n$ and
$s\longrightarrow\infty$, Theorem 7.3 implies

$$
R\ge\frac32n-\frac32\log_2 n-\frac b2-O(1)
=\frac34H-\frac32\log_2 H-\frac b2-O(1).
\tag{DAF.21}
$$

Proof. Catalan and central-binomial asymptotics, credited to the standard
enumeration and Stirling tools, give

$$
T_s=C_{n-1}\binom n{n/2}
\sim\frac{\sqrt2}{4\pi}\frac{8^n}{n^2}.
\tag{DAF.22}
$$

Indeed $C_{n-1}\sim4^{n-1}/(\sqrt\pi(n-1)^{3/2})$ and
$\binom n{n/2}\sim2^n\sqrt{2/(\pi n)}$, giving that constant. By the meaning
of $\sim$, for all sufficiently large multiples of four,
$T_s\ge\sqrt2\,8^n/(8\pi n^2)$. The denominator in (DAF.18) is at most
$22s=(11/2)n$, so

$$
K_s\ge\frac{\sqrt2}{44\pi}\frac{8^n}{n^3}.
\tag{DAF.23}
$$

Taking base-two logarithms and using (DAF.20) gives (DAF.21), with an absolute
additive constant independent of $b$ and $s$. Replacing $n$ by $H/2$ changes
only that constant. The finite bound is (DAF.19); no small-$s$ assertion is
deduced from the asymptotic notation, and no matching optimality is claimed.

